{{define "regions"}}
<h1>Regions</h1>
{{if not .Available}}<div class="note"><strong>Cloud only.</strong> This control plane runs a self-hosted (SQLite) backend, which charges no one — there is no price list to manage here. Pricing and regions are a managed-cloud (Postgres) subsystem.</div>{{else}}
{{if .Flash}}<div class="flash-ok">{{.Flash}}</div>{{end}}
{{if .FlashErr}}<div class="flash-err">{{.FlashErr}}</div>{{end}}

<p class="note">
  <strong>A region is a price, not a placement.</strong> Fly's egress costs
  <strong>six times</strong> more out of Africa ($0.12/GB) than Europe ($0.02/GB), and its
  machines cost more in Johannesburg than Frankfurt. A flat cross-region price loses money on
  every customer in the expensive region — and since we settle in rands, that is the home
  market, not a minority. So each region carries its own egress cost, its own relay price, its
  own free allowance, and its own machine-price multiplier.
  <br><br>
  The relay price <strong>must exceed the egress cost</strong> — the store refuses anything else.
  The free allowance is scaled by <em>cost</em>, not by gigabytes: 25 GB of EU egress costs us
  $0.50, but the same 25 GB out of Africa costs $3.00.
</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr>
  <th>Region</th><th>Egress cost<br>(ours)</th><th>Relay price<br>(theirs)</th><th>Markup</th>
  <th>Free relay<br>(with a box)</th><th>Machine<br>multiplier</th>
  <th>Accounts</th><th>Boxes</th><th>Fly spend</th><th>Est. relay cost</th><th>Status</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
{{range .Regions}}
<tr>
  <td><code>{{.ID}}</code><br><span class="ro">{{.Name}}</span></td>
  <td>{{.EgressCostCentsGB}}c/GB</td>
  <td>{{.RelayPriceCentsGB}}c/GB</td>
  <td>{{.MarkupX}}</td>
  <td>{{.FreeRelayGB}} GB</td>
  <td>{{.ComputeMult}}</td>
  <td>{{.Accounts}}</td>
  <td>{{.Boxes}}</td>
  <td>
    {{/* An honest blank beats a confident zero: if the cost poller has no data,
         say so rather than render $0.00 and imply the region is free. */}}
    {{if .HasSpendData}}${{.SpendUSD}}{{else}}<span class="ro">no data yet</span>{{end}}
  </td>
  <td>${{.EstRelayCostUSD}}<br><span class="ro">{{printf "%.1f" .RelayGBMonth}} GB</span></td>
  <td>{{if .Active}}<span class="ok">open</span>{{else}}<span class="warn">closed</span>{{end}}</td>
</tr>
{{end}}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Open or edit a region</h2>
<p class="note">
  Use the Fly region slug (<code>fra</code>, <code>jnb</code>, <code>ams</code>, <code>syd</code>…).
  Egress cost is what <em>Fly charges us</em> per GB out of that region — look it up on Fly's
  current price list and do not guess: a cost basis that is too low silently inflates every
  margin we compute, in our favour, which is the direction nobody investigates.
  Machine multiplier is in basis points against the European basis (10000 = 1.0x, 18000 = 1.8x).
  Closing a region stops new provisioning there immediately.
</p>
<form method="POST" action="/superadmin/regions" class="stack-form">
  <input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="{{.CSRFToken}}">
  <label>Slug <input type="text" name="id" placeholder="syd" required></label>
  <label>Name <input type="text" name="name" placeholder="Australia (Sydney)" required></label>
  <label>Egress cost (cents/GB, what Fly charges us) <input type="number" name="egress_cost_cents_gb" min="1" required></label>
  <label>Relay price (cents/GB, what we charge — must exceed the cost) <input type="number" name="relay_cents_gb" min="1" required></label>
  <label>Free relay with an enrolled box (GB/month) <input type="number" name="free_relay_gb" min="0" value="25" required></label>
  <label>Machine multiplier (basis points, 10000 = 1.0x) <input type="number" name="compute_mult_bps" min="1" value="10000" required></label>
  <label><input type="checkbox" name="active" value="1" checked> Open for new provisioning</label>
  <button type="submit" class="primary">Save region</button>
</form>
{{end}}{{/* Available */}}
{{end}}
